---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout title="Cascading Ship Date Changes to Every Affected Team - Marker">
  <Navbar />
  <main id="main-content">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-16 md:pt-24 md:pb-24">
      <a href="/case-studies" class="mb-8 inline-flex items-center text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground">
        <svg class="mr-1 h-4 w-4" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"></path>
        </svg>
        All Case Studies
      </a>

      <span class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Premium Menswear</span>
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground md:text-3xl">Cascading Ship Date Changes to Every Affected Team</h1>

      <div class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 p-5 text-sm">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Company</span>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">Premium menswear brand with 10 active vendors and long-tenured supplier relationships</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Team</span>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">Product development, logistics, merchandising, and marketing teams that all depend on accurate ship dates</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Key Challenge</span>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">When a supplier changes a ship date, the update needs to reach logistics, merchants, and marketing — but there was no automated way to cascade the notification</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">The Challenge</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">At this premium menswear brand, a ship date change from a supplier isn't just a logistics problem — it's a chain reaction that touches merchandising, marketing, and retail operations. If a shipment is delayed, the merchant needs to adjust floor plans. Marketing needs to revise campaign timing. Logistics needs to re-plan warehouse capacity. And the product development team needs to update buyers.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">The VP of Product Development described the current process: a supplier sends an email about a delay, and the PD team manually forwards the information to each affected function. If the PD contact is traveling or in meetings, the notification sits in an inbox. If the email is ambiguous about which PO or style is affected, someone has to cross-reference before they can even begin cascading. With the team handling 70-80 emails per day, delay notifications don't always get the priority they need.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">The result is that downstream teams often learn about changes too late to adjust gracefully. Marketing launches a campaign for a product that won't be on shelves. Merchants hold floor space for inventory that's weeks away. The cost isn't in the delay itself — it's in the slow reaction.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">The Approach</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">When Marker detects a ship date change in supplier communication, it automatically identifies the affected purchase orders and the downstream teams that need to know. Notifications are cascaded to logistics, merchandising, and marketing with the relevant context: which styles are affected, the new expected dates, and any penalty clause implications.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">The cascade happens immediately upon detection — not when someone reads the email and decides to forward it. Each notification includes the original supplier communication so recipients can verify the details themselves. For a brand that works with 10 active vendors and maintains long-tenured relationships, this isn't about adversarial tracking. It's about ensuring that a supplier's honest update about a delay reaches everyone who needs to adjust, quickly enough to matter.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">Key Takeaways</h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-muted-foreground">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
            <span>Ship date changes detected in supplier email and automatically cascaded to every affected team</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
            <span>Affected POs and styles identified automatically, eliminating manual cross-referencing</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
            <span>Downstream teams — logistics, merchandising, marketing — alerted with full context to adjust plans immediately</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
            <span>Penalty clause implications surfaced alongside the delay notification for informed decision-making</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-primary/20 bg-primary/5 p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-foreground">See how Marker ensures every team knows about changes the moment they happen.</p>
        <a href="/book-demo" class="mt-4 inline-flex h-10 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-primary px-6 text-sm font-medium text-primary-foreground transition-colors hover:bg-primary/90 focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2">Book a Demo</a>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
